<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Conon</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 389 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> · Also known as Conon of Athens</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Conon was an Athenian admiral of the late 5th and early 4th centuries BCE. He escaped the Athenian defeat at Aegospotami in 405 BCE and took refuge in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and later commanded a fleet for the Persian king. In 394 BCE he and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satrapy">satrap</a> Pharnabazus destroyed the Spartan fleet off Knidos, ending Spartan naval supremacy, and he then helped rebuild the Long Walls of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/conon.jpg" alt="Conon" width="320" height="327" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Published by Guillaume Rouille (1518?-1589) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conon.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Shortly after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-notium">Battle of Notium</a>, Conon took over the Athenian fleet from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcibiades">Alcibiades</a>. The Spartan navarch <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/callicratidas">Callicratidas</a> caught his seventy ships and chased them towards Mytilene on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lesbos">Lesbos</a>, where Conon lost thirty. He drew the other forty onto the beach, but Callicratidas blockaded him by sea and land. Conon slipped a messenger ship out to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, and the relief force defeated the Spartans at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-arginusae">Arginusae</a>, where Callicratidas was killed. In 405 BCE Conon pursued the fleet of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hellespont">Hellespont</a>, where Lysander held a strong position at Lampsacus; the Athenians fell back to Aegospotami, an open beach without harbours. Alcibiades warned them and advised a move to Sestos, but was apparently ignored.</p><p>On the fifth day of the standoff <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lysander">Lysander</a> caught almost the whole Athenian fleet unprepared while the crews had disembarked for their meals. It was generally believed that treachery was involved, but Conon was never implicated. His ship was one of nine that escaped, and he and eight others fled to Evagoras of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, fearing the judgement of the Athenian people. By 397 BCE the satrap Pharnabazus had persuaded the Persian king Artaxerxes to wage war by sea with a fleet of 300 Phoenician and Cypriot ships, and they found Conon on Cyprus to command it. At Cnidus in 394 BCE the Spartans, under the inexperienced Peisander, were overwhelmingly defeated, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a> cities expelled their Spartan garrisons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Conon then returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>. Pharnabazus let him keep part of the fleet and supplied money to fortify <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piraeus">Piraeus</a> and rebuild the long walls. Athens regained its position as a major power in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, though without its empire. The next year the Athenian delegates, Conon among them, protested at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>'s offer to hand over the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and Tiribazus threw them into prison. Ancient writers give conflicting accounts of his end: some say he was sent inland and put to death, others, probably more likely, that he retired to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and died there. His son Timotheus later became another prominent general.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Conon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Off Knidos in 394 BCE <b>Conon's</b> Persian-backed fleet destroyed Spartan sea power.</blockquote><a href="/state/knidos">Knidos</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Cyprus. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-399" aria-label="Athens on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5141 798 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Conon"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5591 1068h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5591 1068h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.98,23.73&amp;year=-399">What was here in 399 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
